## Summary

`layoutNextLine()` and `layoutWithLines()` produce different line breaks for the same prepared text and width when the input contains mixed scripts (Latin + CJK + Arabic RTL + emoji).

This appears to be a separate trigger from #43 (ZWSP + space leading space behavior). Here the inconsistency is in how CJK segment boundaries interact with the line-start normalization logic between the batch and iterator APIs.

## Reproduction

```ts
import { prepareWithSegments, layoutWithLines, layoutNextLine } from '@chenglou/pretext'

const text = 'Hello \u4e16\u754c \u0645\u0631\u062d\u0628\u0627 \ud83c\udf0d test'
const font = '16px Inter'
const width = 80
const lineHeight = 19

const prepared = prepareWithSegments(text, font)

// Batch API
const batch = layoutWithLines(prepared, width, lineHeight)
console.log(batch.lineCount) // 3
console.log(batch.lines.map(l => l.text))
// Line 0: 'Hello '  (start=seg0, end=seg2)
// Line 1: '\u754c \u0645\u0631\u062d\u0628\u0627 '  (start=seg3, end=seg7)
// Line 2: '\ud83c\udf0d test'  (start=seg7, end=seg10)
// Note: '\u4e16' (seg2) is missing from the output

// Iterator API
const lines = []
let cursor = { segmentIndex: 0, graphemeIndex: 0 }
while (true) {
  const line = layoutNextLine(prepared, cursor, width)
  if (line === null) break
  lines.push(line)
  cursor = line.end
}
console.log(lines.length) // 4
console.log(lines.map(l => l.text))
// Line 0: 'Hello '
